message: "Please cite the book when referring to its account of change, emergence, knowledge and action."
title: "Beyond Apparent Paths: The Practice of Seeking Truth from Facts"
type: book
authors:
  - family-names: Guo
    given-names: Jinku
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0000-6600-6171"
url: "https://bookwormseagull1980.github.io/Book-and-Articles/books/beyond-apparent-paths.html"
languages:
  - en
abstract: >-
  Beyond Apparent Paths: The Practice of Seeking Truth from Facts is an
  exploration of how order, life, consciousness, society, and meaning emerge
  from a world in constant change. Moving across physics, biology, neuroscience,
  complexity science, and Eastern and Western philosophy, Guo Jinku develops a
  unified, multi-level way of understanding reality. The book proposes that
  disorder is not merely the opposite of order, but the field of possibilities
  from which order arises; that change is not something added to existence, but
  the very way existence reveals itself; and that every enduring structure is a
  temporary achievement sustained within continuous transformation. Ideas such
  as entropy, symmetry, emergence, evolution, renormalization, consciousness,
  and self-organization are presented not as isolated theories, but as different
  expressions of a shared pattern. Each level of reality possesses its own
  relative autonomy, yet remains connected to the levels below and above it.
  Understanding therefore requires more than reduction: it requires knowing
  when to separate levels, when to connect them, and when to look beyond the
  categories through which we habitually see the world. Neither a conventional
  science textbook nor a purely abstract philosophical treatise, this book is a
  work of integrative thought. Its central concern is methodological: how to
  move beyond apparent paths, return to facts and processes, and discover the
  deeper relations through which the world becomes intelligible.
